WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.726
A trust boundary is a place where data or control crosses between different levels of trust.

2
00:00:06.726 --> 00:00:14.293
The boundary between a browser and a server is one example, but web applications usually have many more.

3
00:00:14.293 --> 00:00:27.745
A server crosses a boundary when it reads from a database, calls a third-party API, consumes a queue message, loads a file, accepts a webhook, or receives data from another internal service.

4
00:00:27.745 --> 00:00:31.108
Internal does not always mean trusted.

5
00:00:31.108 --> 00:00:36.573
Data may have originated outside the organization before it reached an internal component.

6
00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:42.458
Data flow mapping shows where information enters, moves, changes form, and leaves the application.

7
00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:48.763
Start with entry points such as routes, forms, APIs, upload handlers, scheduled jobs, and integrations.

8
00:00:48.763 --> 00:00:55.069
Then track the data through validation, authorization checks, business logic, storage, logging, and response rendering.

9
00:00:55.069 --> 00:01:03.476
Mark sensitive data such as credentials, tokens, personal data, payment data, medical data, source code, business secrets, and authorization decisions.

10
00:01:03.476 --> 00:01:10.623
The map should show not only where data exists, but which code is allowed to use it.

11
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:13.986
Trust boundaries are also role boundaries.

12
00:01:13.986 --> 00:01:25.756
Anonymous users, authenticated users, administrators, service accounts, support staff, partners, and background jobs should not all be able to perform the same actions or see the same data.

13
00:01:25.756 --> 00:01:29.540
Mapping roles with data flow makes review more concrete.

14
00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:46.355
Instead of asking whether the application is secure in general, the team can ask whether this user can reach this data through this route, whether this service call preserves authorization context, and whether this response exposes more information than intended.
